      "latin": "crustulum, crustulī n.",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "small pastry, biscuit",
      "evidence": [
        "Hor. <em>Serm.</em> 1.1.25–26: <span lang=\"la\">ut puerīs ōlim dant crustula blandī doctōrēs, elementa velint ut discere prīma</span>"
      ],
      "comment": "Serviceable for an éclair and the like. Horace’s teachers coax boys with them."
    },
    {
      "latin": "laterculus, laterculī m.",
      "status": "alternative",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "a long, brick-shaped pastry; éclair",
      "evidence": [
        "Cato, Agr. 109; Plaut. Poen. 1.2.115 (Lewis &amp; Short)",
        "Andresian p. 10"
      ],
      "comment": "Properly a small brick or tile; Cato’s and Plautus’s pastry was named for its shape. Andresian’s éclair."
    }
